{"id": "129232", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=129232", "title": "Gunjur", "text": "Gunjur is a town at the Atlantic Ocean in the Gambia.\nUnderstand.\nGunjur is a small rural town far away from the resort areas in the northern part of Gambia. Life is still traditional and the day-to-day life of the local community is almost the same for decades. Maybe now it is going to change because of the access to electricity in 2013.\nDrink.\nYou can't buy any alcohol in Gunjur. Next bar is in Brikama."}
{"id": "129233", "revid": "79572", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=129233", "title": "Algarrobo (Chile)", "text": "Algarrobo is a town of 10,000 people (2012), and a popular summer destination on the central coast of Chile.\nUnderstand.\nAlgarrobo has abundant beaches and beautiful architecture.\nSummer holidays draw in large crowds from nearby Santiago. The San Alfonso del Mar resort north of the city is home to the world's second largest outdoor pool.\nSee.\nThe City center on Avenida Carlos Alessandri, between Playa Las Cadenas to the north and Club de Yates to the south, is home to the greatest commercial, cultural and gastronomic activity in the summer months. Some attractions are, from south to north:\nDo.\nBeaches.\nFrom south to north:\nDrink.\nCentral Chile is famous for its wines and wineries. The Casablanca Wine Valley is some 20 km towards Santiago and home to the best white wine in the country."}
{"id": "129274", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=129274", "title": "Odiham", "text": "Odiham is an historic village of 4,400 people (2011) in Hampshire's Hart district. It is home to a 13th-century castle, and a more modern bat colony.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nOdiham is best accessed from junction 5 of the M3 motorway, 2\u00bd miles (4 km) away. Other routes include the A287 from Farnham or the B3349 from Alton. is at Deer Park View, off the High Street.\nBy train.\nHook railway station, on the Basingstoke to London Waterloo line, is around 3 miles away, connected by bus (lucky number 13) roughly every hour. See schedule here. Several taxi companies serve Hook. Winchfield station is slightly closer to Odiham, but there are no bus connections; an adventurous traveller could walk from there to Odiham on the Three Castles Path (see below).\nOn foot.\nThe is a 60-mile (100-km) walk from Windsor to Winchester via Odiham. Inspired by the journeys of King John between the two cities via the castle he built at Odiham in the time of Magna Carta, it passes through a variety of attractive scenery including parkland, forest, heath, downland, quiet streams and picturesque villages. For more information, see the website, or get a hold of a copy of the book \"The Three Castles Path\" by David Bounds and Dave Ramm ().\nGet around.\nOdiham is best explored on foot."}
